# HTCondor submit file — how many candidates fit in ONE TabPFN predict call on an A100-PCIe-40GB (2026-11-13).
#
# Why. Supervision of 12 Nov on the runtime study: "1000 molecules for timing — does predicting on that sample
# exhaust what is possible with a 40GB A100 GPU? try if more fits in one model.predict step?" The runtime sweep
# (experiments/runtime_study_a100.csv) scored 1,000 candidates and the whole S. aureus pool (about 31,000) in one
# call, but recorded no GPU memory and went no further. src/scripts/runtime_capacity.py makes one predict call for
# 1,000 to 1,000,000 candidates (the pool's molecules repeated), for two representations and two labelled-set
# sizes, and records the time and the peak GPU memory of each call, or the error that stopped it.
#
# Output: experiments/runtime_capacity_a100.csv. The script refuses to start on any other GPU and refuses to
# overwrite its output. It writes a row with status "started" before every attempt and runs the sizes in ascending
# order, so if the job is removed for exceeding its host memory (see request_memory), the file ends with the
# "started" row of the setting it died in and every smaller size is saved.
# Expected run time: about an hour.
#
# Before submitting, on the cluster:
#   1. src/scripts/runtime_capacity.py and cluster/run_gpu_capacity.sh must be there (cluster/sync.sh push);
#   2. experiments/runtime_capacity_a100.csv must not exist;
#   3. mkdir -p runlogs
#
#   condor_submit -dry-run /dev/null cluster/run_gpu_capacity_a100.sub    # validate only
#   condor_submit cluster/run_gpu_capacity_a100.sub
#
# If the dry run rejects the DeviceName expression, use this one instead (the 40 GB A100s):
#   require_gpus = (Capability == 8.0 && GlobalMemoryMb < 50000)
#
# Afterwards, copy experiments/runtime_capacity_a100.csv and the three runlogs/run_gpu_capacity_a100.* files back.
universe                = docker
docker_image            = pytorch/pytorch:2.3.1-cuda12.1-cudnn8-devel
executable              = cluster/run_gpu_capacity.sh
output                  = runlogs/run_gpu_capacity_a100.$(ClusterId).out
error                   = runlogs/run_gpu_capacity_a100.$(ClusterId).err
log                     = runlogs/run_gpu_capacity_a100.$(ClusterId).log

should_transfer_files   = YES
when_to_transfer_output = ON_EXIT
getenv                  = HOME

request_GPUs            = 1
request_CPUs            = 4
# HOST RAM, not GPU memory. The large candidate sets need more than the 7 GB of the runtime sweep: the input matrix
# alone is up to 3 GB (MAX_INPUT_GB in the script) and TabPFN copies it. 12 GB is a compromise: the A100 hosts are
# partitionable and a 16 GB request once left jobs IDLE beside free GPUs. If this job stays IDLE, lower it to 10GB
# (and MAX_INPUT_GB in the script to 2.0); if it is removed for exceeding memory, the CSV shows at which setting.
request_memory          = 12GB
require_gpus            = (DeviceName == "NVIDIA A100-PCIE-40GB")
requirements            = UidDomain == "cs.uni-saarland.de"
+WantGPUHomeMounted     = true
+WantScratchMounted     = true

# As in the runtime sweep. It only widens the machines that accept the job; the GPU model is fixed by require_gpus.
+MaxJobRetirementTime   = 28800

queue 1
